        <h1 class="font-serif font-black text-white text-4xl sm:text-5xl leading-tight">The Best Beaches in Sri Lanka</h1>
        <div class="flex items-center justify-center gap-4 text-white/70 text-sm mt-4">
          <span>June 2025</span><span>·</span><span>10 min read</span>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6 py-16">
      <div class="prose prose-lg max-w-none prose-headings:font-serif prose-headings:text-gray-900 prose-a:text-ceylon-green-700">

        <p>Sri Lanka has over 1,600 kilometres of coastline and somewhere in the range of extraordinary beaches. The challenge isn't finding a good one — it's knowing which one is right for you, when to go, and how the island's two distinct monsoon seasons affect the choice. This guide ranks Sri Lanka's best beaches honestly, by character rather than fame, with notes on who each one suits best.</p>

        <p>The key thing to understand first: Sri Lanka's southwest coast (Galle, Mirissa, Unawatuna) is best from November to April. The east coast (Arugam Bay, Nilaveli, Uppuveli) is best from May to October. The two seasons are almost perfectly complementary — which means Sri Lanka has great beach weather somewhere year-round, but you need to pick the right coast for your travel dates.</p>

        <h2>1. Mirissa</h2>
        <p><a href="/places/mirissa">Mirissa</a> is Sri Lanka's most complete beach — a gorgeous crescent of golden sand backed by coconut palms, with calm, swimmable water from November to April and the island's best whale watching departing from the harbour each morning. The beach has enough character and enough going on (sunset cocktail bars, fresh seafood, a small but good-natured night scene) to keep you entertained for several days. Blue whales pass through these waters between November and April — one of the only places on Earth with reliable blue whale sightings. <strong>Best for:</strong> First-time visitors, whale watching, beach nightlife, couples. <strong>Best time:</strong> December–March.</p>

        <h2>2. Unawatuna</h2>
        <p><a href="/places/unawatuna">Unawatuna</a> is Sri Lanka's most popular beach for good reason: a sheltered horseshoe bay just 6km from Galle Fort, with calm water that's safe for families, good snorkelling on the reef at the far end, and a wide strip of restaurants and guesthouses right on the sand. It does get very busy in peak season (December–January), and the development has crept right to the water's edge, but the fundamental beach experience remains excellent. The nearby Jungle Beach — a 10-minute walk — is smaller, quieter and often strikingly beautiful. <strong>Best for:</strong> Families, first-time visitors, combining with Galle Fort. <strong>Best time:</strong> November–April.</p>

        <h2>3. Nilaveli</h2>
        <p><a href="/places/nilaveli">Nilaveli</a> is Sri Lanka's east coast showpiece — a long, wide stretch of powder-white sand near Trincomalee, with startlingly clear water and almost no development beyond a handful of quiet guesthouses and resorts. The beach itself is magnificent, and the reef just offshore at Pigeon Island Marine National Park offers the island's best snorkelling — blacktip reef sharks, turtles and dense coral gardens visible in shallow water. The east coast's best-kept secret is that this beach is open and beautiful while the south coast is being rained on. <strong>Best for:</strong> Snorkelling, quiet beach days, east coast explorers. <strong>Best time:</strong> May–September.</p>

        <h2>4. Arugam Bay</h2>
        <p><a href="/places/arugam-bay">Arugam Bay</a> operates on its own terms. The Main Point break is one of the best right-hand point breaks in Asia, drawing serious surfers from around the world between May and October, but the bay has a barefoot, unhurried quality that makes it appealing even to non-surfers. Tuk-tuk rides to nearby lagoons and elephant sightings, Sri Lankan buffalo on the beach at sunset, and a line of unpretentious cafes along the strip — Arugam Bay has retained its charm despite growing steadily more popular. <strong>Best for:</strong> Intermediate to advanced surfers, east coast road trips, laid-back travellers. <strong>Best time:</strong> May–October.</p>

        <h2>5. Hiriketiya</h2>
        <p><a href="/places/hiriketiya">Hiriketiya</a> is the south coast's most photogenic beach — a perfect horseshoe cove where the water is warm, the waves are gentle enough for beginners, and the palm-shaded beach is ringed by a small collection of surf camps, yoga studios and excellent cafes. It's also crowded in peak season, which is both its energy and its drawback. Come for the surf lesson, the smoothie bowl, the Instagram opportunity, and the genuinely beautiful natural setting. <strong>Best for:</strong> Beginner surfers, yoga retreats, digital nomads. <strong>Best time:</strong> November–April.</p>

        <h2>6. Uppuveli</h2>
        <p><a href="/places/uppuveli">Uppuveli</a> sits just north of Trincomalee and offers everything Nilaveli does — white sand, clear water, excellent snorkelling — in a slightly more local and lived-in setting. There are fewer resort-style hotels and more guesthouses run by families who have been here for decades. The beach itself is beautiful and the water is excellent for swimming throughout the east coast season. A war memorial cemetery nearby is a sobering reminder of Trincomalee's strategic history. <strong>Best for:</strong> East coast exploration, budget travellers, avoiding crowds. <strong>Best time:</strong> May–September.</p>

        <h2>7. Hikkaduwa</h2>
        <p><a href="/places/hikkaduwa">Hikkaduwa</a> was Sri Lanka's original beach resort — the first stretch of coastline that international backpackers discovered in the 1970s and 1980s, and it has never entirely shaken that legacy. The coral reef just offshore is the reason to come: snorkelling directly from the beach, you'll find turtles, reef fish and decent coral in water shallow enough for confident swimmers. The surf breaks are also good. The beach strip is developed and commercial in parts, but the reef alone is worth it. <strong>Best for:</strong> Reef snorkelling, surf, budget accommodation. <strong>Best time:</strong> November–April.</p>

        <h2>8. Tangalle</h2>
        <p><a href="/places/tangalle">Tangalle</a>'s beaches are the south coast's wildest — long, windswept stretches of golden sand with crashing surf, strong currents (swimming is often only safe in the lagoon areas) and a remote, undeveloped feel that is increasingly rare on this coastline. Sea turtles nest here between April and June. The area around Tangalle has some of the south coast's finest boutique accommodation, tucked behind the beaches in coconut gardens and clifftop positions. <strong>Best for:</strong> Boutique hotel stays, turtle nesting, south coast road trips. <strong>Best time:</strong> November–March (avoid the monsoon, May–September).</p>

