        <h2>9. Talpe (Thalpe)</h2>
        <p><a href="/places/talpe">Talpe</a> (also spelled Thalpe) is 10km east of Galle and represents the south coast at its most quietly luxurious. The ancient rock pools — hand-cut into the coral reef over 50 years ago, filling and draining with the tides — are one of Sri Lanka's most unusual natural features. The stilt fishermen who balance on poles above the surf at sunrise are the image of Sri Lanka you see in magazines. The guesthouses and boutique villas here are among the finest on the coast. <strong>Best for:</strong> Boutique villa stays, photography, stilt fishermen. <strong>Best time:</strong> November–April.</p>

        <h2>10. Pasikuda</h2>
        <p><a href="/places/pasikuda">Pasikuda</a> is the east coast's most famous beach, and the reason is immediately obvious: the lagoon here is so shallow — barely waist-deep for 300 metres from shore — and so turquoise that it looks like a swimming pool the size of a football pitch. It's one of the most beautiful beach environments in Sri Lanka, lined with resort hotels that have invested heavily in the location. The snorkelling on the fringing reef is excellent. <strong>Best for:</strong> Families, luxury resorts, east coast first-timers. <strong>Best time:</strong> April–September.</p>

        <h2>11. Weligama</h2>
        <p><a href="/places/weligama">Weligama</a>'s wide, sweeping bay is the best place in Sri Lanka to learn to surf. The beach break is gentle, consistent and forgiving, and there are dozens of surf schools offering lessons at competitive prices. The bay is also long enough that beginners and intermediate surfers can spread out comfortably. Beyond surfing, Weligama is a pleasant town with good food options and a convenient base between Galle and Mirissa. <strong>Best for:</strong> Beginner surfers, learning to surf, south coast road trips. <strong>Best time:</strong> November–April.</p>

        <h2>12. Bentota</h2>
        <p><a href="/places/bentota">Bentota</a> is Sri Lanka's luxury beach resort town — a wide sweep of sand on the west coast backed by international hotels and water sports operators working the calm Bentota Lagoon behind the beach. Jet skiing, banana boats, windsurfing, river safaris through the lagoon mangroves — Bentota is the most active of Sri Lanka's beach destinations, and the best option for families who want resort infrastructure with beach access. <strong>Best for:</strong> Luxury resort stays, water sports, families wanting amenities. <strong>Best time:</strong> November–April.</p>

        <h2>13. Madiha</h2>
        <p><a href="/places/madiha">Madiha</a> sits between Mirissa and Matara — just far enough from its famous neighbours to have missed the development wave. The reef close to shore shelters sea turtles year-round, and the snorkelling is accessible directly from the beach. Small guesthouses line the sand and the pace is genuinely unhurried. If you want the south coast experience without the crowds, Madiha is the answer. <strong>Best for:</strong> Snorkelling, sea turtles, quiet beach days. <strong>Best time:</strong> December–April.</p>

        <h2>14. Kalpitiya</h2>
        <p><a href="/places/kalpitiya">Kalpitiya</a> is unlike anywhere else on the Sri Lanka coast — a remote peninsula on the northwest, where a vast lagoon behind the sand strip creates ideal conditions for kitesurfing, and spinner dolphins number in the thousands offshore. The beach itself is wild and undeveloped; the appeal is the wind, the water and the sense of being genuinely far from the tourist circuit. <strong>Best for:</strong> Kitesurfers, dolphin watching, off-the-beaten-track travel. <strong>Best time:</strong> May–October (the kitesurfing season).</p>

        <h2>15. Mawella Bay</h2>
        <p><a href="/places/mawella">Mawella Bay</a> is the south coast's best-kept secret — a sheltered horseshoe bay 7km west of Tangalle where a natural breakwater creates a calm lagoon ideal for swimming and kayaking, while the fishing village behind the beach remains entirely unaffected by tourism. The morning return of the fishing fleet — colourful outriggers arriving through the surf at 6am — is one of the most vivid scenes on the south coast. <strong>Best for:</strong> Quiet beach days, kayaking, fishing village photography. <strong>Best time:</strong> November–April.</p>

        <h2>East Coast vs South Coast: The Seasonal Switch</h2>
        <p>The most important thing to know about Sri Lanka's beaches is the monsoon calendar. The <strong>southwest monsoon</strong> (May–September) brings rain and rough seas to the south and west coasts — Mirissa, Hikkaduwa, Unawatuna and Bentota are all affected. During the same months, the <strong>east coast</strong> — Arugam Bay, Nilaveli, Uppuveli, Pasikuda — is sunny, calm and at its best. Then from October, the pattern reverses: the <strong>northeast monsoon</strong> affects the east coast, and the south coast dries out for its November–April peak season. There is no bad time to be in Sri Lanka — you just need to be on the right coast.</p>

